        { "$comment": "★★ P58 [[D-CSUBSET-PER-MEMBER-PACKED]]: GNU `packed` written on ONE struct/union MEMBER-DECLARATOR — `struct S { char a; int z __attribute__((packed)); double d; };` — which lowers THAT member's baseline alignment to 1 and leaves its siblings, AND the aggregate's own size and alignment, alone. The sink is the composite's `fieldPacked` channel in the type interner, read by `computeLayout` through the SAME `clampedBaselineAlign` the whole-composite `packed` and `#pragma pack(N)` already meet at.\n★★★ THIS NAME LEFT THE `none` ROW, AND THAT MOVE IS THE WHOLE FIX. `none` asserts 'KNOWN vocabulary, consumed elsewhere or deliberately inert', and for `packed` the 'elsewhere' is `semantics.packed` / `scanCompositePacked` — TRUE at the composite position and FALSE at the member position. TF-C73 opened the member attribute position arguing that 'one attribute does not mean two things depending on which side of the declarator it is written'; `packed` is precisely that attribute, and for every cycle since, the member spelling has PARSED and been DROPPED IN SILENCE. ✔MEASURED at base 01642ee3 through the shipped CLI: `struct { char a; int z __attribute__((packed)); double d; }` compiled rc 0 with ZERO diagnostics and placed `z` at offset 4, where gcc 13.3.0 and clang 18.1.3 both place it at 1 — identical sizeof (16), identical _Alignof (8), wrong bytes. A runnable witness exited 45 on pe64-x86_64, elf64-x86_64 and elf64-aarch64 alike, against 42 on six working reference arms.\n★★ REFERENCES, EACH PROBED SEPARATELY, ZERO DISAGREEMENTS ACROSS BOTH BYTE ORDERS: gcc 13.3.0 and clang 18.1.3 on x86_64, on aarch64 under qemu, and on BIG-ENDIAN s390x under qemu; mingw-w64 gcc 13.2.0 agrees on every non-bit-field shape. MSVC 19.51.36252 implements no `__attribute__` in ANY position (C2146/C2061/C2059 at /std:c11, /std:c17 AND /std:clatest) and therefore ABSTAINS — an abstention is not agreement, and two working references make the GNU spelling REQUIRED.\n★ `appliesTo: [\"variable\"]` is MEASURED, not a shrug. A struct member is `kind: \"variable\"` (this file's own `structField` declaration row), so the member case passes the shared decl-kind gate; a FUNCTION and a TYPEDEF do not, and gcc WARNS `'packed' attribute ignored [-Wattributes]` on exactly those two (✔MEASURED, and `_Alignof` of a packed typedef's type stays 4), which is what the gate then reports. The four-kind `DeclarationKind` vocabulary CANNOT separate a MEMBER from a plain OBJECT — both are `variable` — so an object stays silent (clang's posture; both references emit identical bytes there) and the member-only-ness lives in the SINK, the only place that can express it.\n⚠ NOT `none`, and not a grammar change either: `structField`/`unionField` have carried a trailing `structMemberAttrList` since TF-C73 and `structMemberDeclSpecifier(Tail)` already admits `attrSpec`, so BOTH member positions already parsed. The two positions carry DIFFERENT grains and both are already wired — ✔MEASURED on a shape built to discriminate, the after-declarator run appertains to the LAST declarator only (12/align 2/p@2 q@4) and the specifier-prefix run to EVERY declarator (11/align 1/p@1 q@3), against an undecorated control of 16/align 8/p@2 q@8.", "names": ["packed"], "appliesTo": ["variable"], "withinDeclarator": "type", "effect": "packField" },
